 **Mississippi — the 2024–2026 data gap.** Mississippi is fully implemented and validated, but if you query it you'll notice contributions and expenditures cut off cleanly after 2023, with recent years all but empty. This is **not** a scraper bug. It was investigated by querying the live source API directly with explicit date ranges, which returns the same near-empty result — ruling out silent truncation. The root cause is state law: Mississippi does not require electronic filing until **January 1, 2027** ([HB1334, 2025 Regular Session](https://legiscan.com/MS/bill/HB1334/2025)). Until then, candidates and committees may file on paper, and those filings aren't reliably transcribed into the searchable system this pipeline reads from. The window should backfill naturally once mandatory e-filing begins; re-scrapes after that date should be checked for a sudden jump in 2024–2026 coverage. Full detail in [docs/states/mississippi.md](docs/states/mississippi.md).
 
-**Kansas and Montana — in development.** These are not production-ready and their output (if any) should be treated as provisional:
+**Kansas — headed only, and rate-limited.** Kansas reads the Secretary of State's CFR Examiner directly (`sos.ks.gov/elections/cfr_viewer/`) rather than downloading ~3,600 PDFs. Two operational constraints, both confirmed on live runs:
+
+- **It must run headed.** CloudFront serves headless Chrome a "403 ERROR / Request blocked" page instead of the site; a visible Chrome window on the same machine and IP works fine. So KS can't run unattended on a headless box without a virtual display — exclude it from daemon runs or give those runs a display.
+- **The site rate-limits sustained scraping.** A full run was cut off ~54 candidates in. The scraper pauses between candidates (`--delay`, default 0.5s; use 1–2s if you get blocked), and on a block it backs off up to ~30 minutes, then re-submits the search and pages back to where it stopped. Runs are resumable: Ctrl-C is safe, and re-running the same command skips everything already in the manifest.
+
+A full sweep is 78 searches across candidate committees, PACs and party committees, and takes hours.
+
+**Know the coverage limit before using this data.** Kansas accepts filings on paper as well as electronically, and a paper filing is published only as a scanned PDF — there is no HTML report behind it, and nothing itemized to parse. The two are told apart in the results grid: an e-filed row puts the filer's name inside the link, a paper-only row leaves that link empty and prints the name beside it. The scraper recognises the second kind, counts it as `paper-only`, and moves on.
+
+The consequence: **a candidate, PAC or party committee that filed only on paper does not appear in the cleaned data at all**, and one that filed both ways appears with only its e-filed periods. So a $0 total in this dataset means "nothing e-filed", not "raised nothing". The gap is largest in older cycles — 771 of 1,695 rows in the House 2016 search were paper-only — and the `paper-only skipped` count in each run's summary is the honest size of it. If you need those filings, the PDFs are still on the site and the old `kansas_pdf_legacy.py` scraper/parser (unwired) show one way to read them.
 
-- **Kansas** has a working original scraper/parser (`kansas.py`) but is mid-rewrite — `kansas_v2.py` is the in-progress replacement.
-- **Montana** has its source fully reverse-engineered and documented (`docs/states/montana.md`), but the scraper and parser are not yet wired into the pipeline.
